Types of Operating System

An Operating System can be classified based on:

How it executes jobs
How many users it supports
How many tasks it runs
How resources are shared
How quickly responses are needed
Big Picture
Types of Operating Systems

1. Batch OS
2. Multiprogramming OS
3. Multitasking OS
4. Time-Sharing OS
5. Multiuser OS
6. Multiprocessing OS
7. Distributed OS
8. Network OS
9. Real-Time OS
10. Embedded OS
11. Mobile OS

Let's understand them one by one.

1. Batch Operating System

This is one of the oldest OS types.

Problem Before Batch OS

In early computers:

User 1 → Run Program
Wait
User 2 → Run Program
Wait
User 3 → Run Program

Huge waste of time.

Solution

Users submitted jobs together.

OS collected them into a batch.

Then executed them one after another automatically.

Example

Imagine a teacher checking exam copies.

Instead of:

Check 1 copy
Student arrives
Check another copy

Teacher collects:

100 answer sheets

Then checks them continuously.

That's Batch Processing.

Characteristics

✔ Jobs grouped into batches

✔ No direct user interaction

✔ High throughput

❌ Long waiting time

❌ No immediate response

Real-Life Examples
Salary Processing

Company computes salaries for:

10,000 employees

at night.

Electricity Bill Generation

Generate lakhs of bills together.

Bank Interest Calculation

Calculate monthly interest for all accounts.

2. Multiprogramming Operating System

Very important.

Problem

Suppose one process is waiting for disk.

CPU becomes idle.

Waste.

Solution

Keep multiple programs in memory.

If one waits:

Run another.

Example:

Chrome → Waiting

CPU would be idle.

Instead:

VS Code → Running
Goal

Keep CPU busy.

Example

You have:

Chrome
Spotify
VS Code

all loaded in RAM.

OS switches among them.

Characteristics

✔ Better CPU utilization

✔ Multiple programs in memory

✔ Less CPU idle time

3. Multitasking Operating System

Most common OS today.

Definition

Allows multiple tasks to run apparently simultaneously.

Example

You are:

Listening to Spotify
Browsing Chrome
Writing code
Downloading files

at the same time.

Actually:

CPU rapidly switches.

Chrome
Spotify
VS Code
Chrome
Spotify
Examples
Microsoft Windows
Ubuntu
macOS
4. Time-Sharing Operating System

Interview favorite.

Idea

Each process gets a small time slice.

Example:

Chrome → 5 ms
VS Code → 5 ms
Spotify → 5 ms

Then repeat.

Users feel:

Everything is running simultaneously.
Real-Life Example

Teacher gives:

1 minute

to each student for asking questions.

Nobody monopolizes the class.

Characteristics

✔ Fast response

✔ Fair CPU sharing

✔ Interactive systems

Examples
Linux
Unix
Windows
Multiprogramming vs Time-Sharing

Many students confuse these.

Multiprogramming

Goal:

Keep CPU Busy
Time-Sharing

Goal:

Give Fast Response To Users
5. Multiuser Operating System

Allows multiple users to use the same computer system.

Example

University Server

Users:

Student A
Student B
Student C

All use same server.

OS keeps user data separate.

Examples
Unix
Linux
Mainframe systems
Real-Life Example

PG Hostel

Many students.

Same building.

Different rooms.

6. Multiprocessing Operating System

Very important today.

Definition

Uses multiple CPUs or multiple cores.

Example:

Your laptop:

8 Cores

Instead of one CPU:

Core 1
Core 2
Core 3
...

Processes can execute truly in parallel.

Example

While:

Chrome runs on Core 1
VS Code on Core 2
Spotify on Core 3
Characteristics

✔ Higher speed

✔ Better throughput

✔ Parallel execution

Examples

Modern:

Windows
Linux
macOS
Multitasking vs Multiprocessing
Multitasking

One CPU rapidly switches.

Multiprocessing

Multiple CPUs/Cores work simultaneously.

7. Distributed Operating System

Advanced topic.

Multiple computers appear as one system.

Example

Suppose:

Computer A
Computer B
Computer C

connected together.

Users see:

One Large System

Work gets distributed.

Real-Life Example

Group project.

Different students do different parts.

Final result appears as one project.

Applications
Scientific computing
Cloud infrastructure
Supercomputers
8. Network Operating System

Different from Distributed OS.

Interviewers ask this.

Computers remain independent.

They simply communicate through network.

Example

Office Network

Computer A
Computer B
Computer C

Each has its own OS.

But they share:

Files
Printers
Resources

through network.

Examples
Windows Server
Linux Servers
Difference
Distributed OS

Looks like one computer.

Network OS

Looks like many computers connected together.

9. Real-Time Operating System (RTOS)

Extremely important.

Definition

Response must occur within a fixed deadline.

Failure to respond on time can be dangerous.

Example

Airbag System

When accident occurs:

Airbag must open immediately.

Not after 5 seconds.

Examples
Aircraft control
Medical devices
Industrial robots
Nuclear systems
Types
Hard Real-Time

Missing deadline unacceptable.

Example:

Aircraft control.

Soft Real-Time

Occasional delay acceptable.

Example:

Video streaming.

Examples of RTOS
VxWorks
QNX
FreeRTOS
10. Embedded Operating System

Used inside dedicated devices.

Examples:

Washing Machine
Smart TV
ATM
Microwave
Printer

Usually designed for one purpose.

Characteristics

✔ Small size

✔ Low memory usage

✔ Specific functionality

11. Mobile Operating System

Designed for smartphones and tablets.

Examples:

Android
iOS

Features:

Touch support
Battery optimization
Mobile networking
Sensors
